From: retroforge
Subject: [PATCH] drm: drive a secondary output next to the main one

Dual-screen boards (AYN Thor / Thor Lite, AYANEO Pocket DS) have a
second internal panel. --secondary-output <connector> makes gamescope
drive it as well, instead of leaving it dark.

The secondary output owns its connector, a CRTC, a primary plane and
its preferred mode. It keeps the CRTC and plane already driving the
connector (the boot console leaves every panel lit; moving it would
leave that CRTC active with no connector, which the kernel rejects),
else takes the last free ones, and it is refused when the main output
would be left without a CRTC or primary plane. The main output never
touches them: its connector/CRTC/plane selection skips them, its
modesets leave them alone, foreign-plane cleanup treats that CRTC as
ours, and the plane is not registered with libliftoff (liftoff zeroes
every plane it knows and does not use, on every apply).

It is committed with its own non-blocking atomic requests, never
together with the main output: the panels are not genlocked, and a
joint commit would hold the main flip until the other panel's vblank.
Its properties are written raw, not through CDRMAtomicProperty, whose
pending/current state the main path commits and rolls back for every
object at once. Its flip events carry their own user data, so the flip
handler routes them before touching the main output's present context.
A commit never waits: while a flip is pending the next one is refused.
A pause (VT switch) or a forced modeset makes its next commit a modeset
again, re-issued from the main output's state poll; finish_drm drops
its objects before freeing them.

Nothing paints it yet: it shows black. ConVar
drm_debug_secondary_pattern flips it between two greys every vblank and
logs the achieved flip rate, to exercise the commit path. Without
--secondary-output nothing changes.

(retroforge: dual-screen boards, step 2 of the dual-output plan, 2026-10-12.
HW spike: separate commits on the two CRTCs of the msm DPU flip
independently, DSI-1 at 120 Hz and DSI-2 at 60 Hz, no EBUSY.)
